# NSG — node heights, antenna spacing, cell selection, the DynamicTtt curve and
# the RU power profile. Second batch of the parameter-parity push.
#
# Four of these needed NO C++ at all: they are plain LteEnbRrc /
# MmWaveRadioEnergyModelEnb attributes that nothing was passing. The other two
# are small scenario edits. The pattern of this whole effort holds — most of
# what looked missing was reachable, just unrouted.
#
# ── WIRED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# * NODE HEIGHTS. NsgParsePositions hardcoded z=3 for EVERY node, so
#   base_stations[].position.z and the (new) ue_groups[].height_m were both
#   inert: a 32 m macro mast, a 1.5 m handset and a 60 m drone were the same
#   height to the channel model. That silently flattens every elevation-
#   dependent effect — including the antenna downtilt shipped in Phase 2c, which
#   was being measured against a geometry where nothing had a height.
#   Positions now carry an optional third field; two-field records still parse
#   and keep the old 3 m.
# * ANTENNA ELEMENT SPACING -> UniformPlanarArray::AntennaHorizontal/
#   VerticalSpacing, as fields 6 and 7 of the per-gNB antenna spec (0.5
#   wavelengths default, the 3GPP value). Tighter couples the elements and
#   broadens the beam; wider sharpens it and raises grating lobes.
# * settings.outage_threshold_db -> LteEnbRrc::OutageThreshold. The serving-SINR
#   floor below which a UE gives up on mmWave and falls back to the LTE anchor.
#   It is also the floor ho_a2_threshold_db (Phase 2b) has to sit above to be
#   reachable at all — we shipped the A2 lever without exposing the boundary
#   that constrains it.
# * settings.q_rx_lev_min_dbm -> LteEnbRrc::QRxLevMin. 3GPP TS 36.133 cell
#   (re)selection: the minimum RSRP before a UE may camp. The reselection row
#   the VIAVI parity read listed as missing.
# * THE DynamicTtt CURVE -> MinDynTttValue / MaxDynTttValue / MinDiffValue /
#   MaxDiffValue. DynamicTtt is the DEFAULT handover mode and only the FixedTtt
#   value was exposed, so the mode the engine actually runs had none of its four
#   parameters reachable. This is the shape of the whole curve: how long to wait
#   when a neighbour is marginally better vs overwhelmingly better.
# * RU POWER PROFILE -> TxCurrentA / RxCurrentA / DeepSleepA (a legacy attribute
#   name for active-idle). The VIAVI energy profile's standby-watts /
#   fixed-consumption side, which the Phase 2a sleep depths step DOWN from — we
#   had the depths without the baseline they descend from. Sent only when the
#   config overrides them, so the engine's built-in model stays in charge.
#
# ── Trap this cost a rebuild ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The scripts were patched with a Python one-liner opened in text mode on
# Windows, which rewrote both run_*.sh with CRLF. bash then died on
# `cleanup() {\r` and ns-3 never launched, so all five variants came back
# "no_data" with no scenario log at all — a failure that looks like an engine
# fault and is a line-ending. When editing files that live in WSL from the
# Windows side, write with newline="\n" (or run sed -i 's/\r$//' after).
#
# ── UNIT TRAP: QRxLevMin is NOT in dBm ─────────────────────────────────────
# ns-3 takes it in the 3GPP TS 36.133 SIGNALLED unit — 2 dB steps, checker range
# -70..-22, meaning -140..-44 dBm. The schema field is in dBm (which is what a
# user thinks in), so the platform divides by 2. Passing dBm straight through is
# REJECTED by the attribute checker, which aborts ns-3 before it starts — and
# that surfaces as the engine returning "no_data" with no scenario log, i.e. it
# looks like an engine fault, not like a bad parameter. Worth remembering for
# every future pass-through: a rejected attribute is indistinguishable from a
# dead engine unless you read /tmp/scn0.log.
#
# ── Measured (2026-07-26, engines/nsoran/test_geometry_mro.py, PASS) ───────
# 3 cells at 32/18/25 m, 6 UEs, 50 Mbps/UE offered, shadowing on:
#
#   A  UE height 1.5 m                    17.77 Mbps/UE   4475 W   z seen [1.5]
#   B  UE height 60 m (drone fleet)        9.59            6748 W   z seen [60.0]
#   C  element spacing 0.9 wavelengths    14.23            5096 W
#   D  outage -12 dB, QRxLevMin -60,      17.76            4475 W
#      DynamicTtt curve 40-200 ms
#   E  RU idle current 400 A              17.75            7088 W  (+58.4%)
#
# * Heights are asserted against the scenario's OWN position trace (it prints
#   x:y:z per UE), so this is not a plumbing claim — the nodes are where the
#   config put them. Every node used to report z=3.0.
# * A 60 m drone fleet measures 9.59 vs 17.77 Mbps/UE at 1.5 m on identical
#   geometry: real elevation-dependent propagation, which was impossible to see
#   while every node sat at the same height.
# * The LteEnbRrc attributes are pass-through, and ns-3 aborts on a value its
#   checker rejects, so a healthy run IS the check that the value was accepted.
# * RU idle current 400 A vs the model's ~61 A: 4475 -> 7088 W (+58.4%) with
#   throughput unchanged, which is the right signature for an idle-power knob.
#   Doubling it (to 120 A) moved power only ~9% — on a loaded network the cells
#   are transmitting most of the time and the idle term is a minority share, so
#   a small multiplier lands inside the harness's own run-to-run noise. Assert
#   with a big multiplier, or build an idle-cell geometry.
